    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Venice grew from lagoon communities of fishermen and refugees under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Byzantine Exarchate of Ravenna</a> into an independent republic whose ducal seat moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rialto">Rialto</a> in the early 9th century.</li><li>Its fleets, its trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and its share of the Byzantine lands after 1204 made it, by the late 13th century, the most prosperous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</li><li>The sea route round the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, the Ottoman wars and the plagues wore its power down, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> ended the Republic in May 1797.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The word ghetto is originally Venetian: it names the area of Venice in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were compelled to live under the Republic.</p></aside>
    </section>

