    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">421</span><span class="rf-text">Tradition dates the founding to the dedication of the church of San Giacomo on the islet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rialto">Rialto</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">810</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy">Pepin of Italy</a> withdraws after a six-month siege of the lagoon, in which his army is ravaged by disease</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">828</span><span class="rf-text">Relics claimed to be those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">St Mark</a> the Evangelist are brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> to the new basilica</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1204</span><span class="rf-text">Venetians and French crusaders capture and sack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and Venice takes territories in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1380</span><span class="rf-text">The Venetians destroy the Genoese fleet at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chioggia">Chioggia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1509</span><span class="rf-text">Venice is defeated at Agnadello by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-cambrai">League of Cambrai</a> and recovers its mainland lands over the next seven years</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1630</span><span class="rf-text">Plague kills about a third of the population</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1797</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> conquers Venice and the Republic ends, and the city passes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aquileia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aquileia.jpg" alt="Aquileia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_Patriarcale_(Aquileia)_-_Exterior.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ThePhotografer, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Venice stands on a group of islands in the shallow Venetian Lagoon, an enclosed bay at the head of the Adriatic between the mouths of the Po and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piave">Piave</a>. The islands rest on alluvial silt washed down from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. No surviving historical records deal directly with the founding or building of the city. Tradition and the available evidence have led several historians to agree that the first population was made up of refugees from nearby Roman cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>, Treviso, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altinum">Altinum</a> and Concordia, and from the undefended countryside, who were fleeing successive Germanic and Hun invasions. Late Roman sources mention fishermen on the islands, called incolae lacunae, "lagoon dwellers".</p><p>The traditional founding is the dedication of the church of San Giacomo on the islet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rialto">Rialto</a> (Rivoalto, "High Shore"), said to have taken place at noon on 25 March 421. One account adds that authorities from Padua founded the city as a trading post. The church itself is dated no earlier than the 11th century and no later than the mid-12th, so the date remains a tradition. Archaeology shows that the island of Olivolo, now San Pietro di Castello, was inhabited in the 5th century, and the Rialto islands were not properly urbanised until the 9th. The settlers learned to build by driving closely spaced alder piles into the mud and sand until they reached compressed clay, and laid plates of Istrian limestone on top.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Venice&#8221; cites where it covers Origins:</p><ul><li>John Julius Norwich, A History of Venice (Alfred A. Knopf, 1982)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius.jpg" alt="Aldus Manutius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius">Aldus Manutius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_d%27Alde_Manuce,_en_buste,_de_profil_dirig%C3%A9_%C3%A0_droite_-_estampe_-_btv1b84007044_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rota, Martinus Sibenicensis (1520?-1583). Graveur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the 9th to the 12th century Venice developed into a maritime power, one of the Italian maritime republics. Its position at the head of the Adriatic made its naval and commercial strength almost invulnerable, and its reliable gold ducat supported confidence in its trade. Early prosperity probably owed much to a trade in slaves taken in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. In the 12th century the Republic built a large shipyard, the Arsenal, which several historians regard as the first factory in history. After 1204 it held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> and the islands of the Duchy of the Archipelago. It dominated the trade in salt and was a centre of commerce in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, grain and spice.</p><p>By the late 13th century Venice was the most prosperous city in Europe. At the peak of its power, in 1410, it had a navy of some 3,300 ships manned by 36,000 men. Its population was 60,000 in 1000, 80,000 in 1200 and between 110,000 and 180,000 in 1300, about 170,000 in the mid-1500s and close to 200,000 by 1600. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> produced silks, brocades, goldsmith work, armour and glass. By 1500 the city had 417 printers, and the Aldine Press of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius">Aldus Manutius</a>, founded in 1494, printed the first work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> in 1497.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Venice&#8221; cites where it covers Expansion, Demographics:</p><ul><li>Thomas F. Madden, Enrico Dandolo and the Rise of Venice (Johns Hopkins University Press, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/francesco-foscari"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-foscari.jpg" alt="Francesco Foscari" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/francesco-foscari">Francesco Foscari</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Portrait_of_the_Venecian_doge_Francesco_Foscari_by_Lazzaro_Bastiani_-_Correr_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Didier Descouens, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Venice began as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Byzantine Exarchate of Ravenna</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> conquered most of the exarchate in 751, it was an increasingly autonomous Byzantine outpost. Tradition names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-lucio-anafesto">Paolo Lucio Anafesto</a> as the first doge in 697, on the authority of a chronicle written about 1008; some modern historians identify him with the exarch Paul. At about the time of the rising of 726 the people of the lagoon elected their own leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orso-ipato">Ursus</a>, the first of 117 doges, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> confirmed as dux and hypatus. The ducal seat moved from Malamocco to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rialto">Rialto</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnello-participazio">Agnello Particiaco</a> (811 to 827). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s son Pepin besieged the lagoon in 810 and failed, and in 814 an agreement between Charlemagne and the Byzantine emperor Nicephorus recognised Venice as Byzantine territory with trading rights on the Adriatic coast.</p><p>By the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the city was governed by a Great Council of noble families, which appointed officials and elected a Senate of 200 to 300 members; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-ten">Council of Ten</a> controlled much of the administration, and the doge, chosen from the Great Council, usually held office until his death. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-foscari">Francesco Foscari</a> (1423 to 1457) the Republic reached its greatest territorial extent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> conquered Venice on 12 May 1797, and the Austrians took control of the city on 18 January 1798. It joined Napoleon's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1805, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1814, and became part of the Kingdom of Italy in 1866, after a revolt under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniele-manin">Daniele Manin</a> in 1848 had been crushed in 1849.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Venice&#8221; cites where it covers Sestieri:</p><ul><li>Joanne Ferraro, Venice: History Of The Floating City (Cambridge University Press, 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/titian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/titian.jpg" alt="Titian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titian_-_Self-portrait_(Museo_del_Prado).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The historic city is divided into six districts, the sestieri of Cannaregio, Castello, Dorsoduro, San Marco, San Polo and Santa Croce, created about 1170. They contain parishes, 70 in 1033, reduced under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and now 38. Each parish chose its patron saint, held its own festivals, gathered round its own market and built its own bell tower. Most Venetians were Roman Catholics, but the state was notable for its freedom from religious fanaticism and executed nobody for heresy during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. It was threatened with the interdict several times and twice suffered it, the second time in 1606 under Pope Paul V. The long link with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> left an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> presence, and Armenian merchants from Julfa were leading traders in the 17th century.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Venice were compelled to live in the Ghetto. The first complete and uncensored printed edition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> was printed in Venice by Daniel Bomberg in 1523, and Napoleon, on conquering the city, removed the gates of the Ghetto. Venetian Gothic combined the lancet arch with the ogee arch under Byzantine and Ottoman influence, and the Doge's Palace and the Ca' d'Oro are its chief examples. Early masters of Venetian painting were the Bellini and Vivarini families, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgione">Giorgione</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tintoretto">Tintoretto</a> and Veronese. The glass industry moved to the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murano">Murano</a> toward the end of the 13th century, and Byzantine craftsmen who fled in 1204 and 1453 helped its development.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/andrea-gritti"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrea-gritti.jpg" alt="Andrea Gritti" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/andrea-gritti">Andrea Gritti</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_del_Doge_Andrea_Gritti_-_Tiziano_059.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> devastated Venice in 1348. Plague returned between 1575 and 1577 and killed some 50,000 people in three years; the Festa del Redentore began as thanks for its end. The plague of 1630 killed a third of the city's 150,000 citizens, and the church of Santa Maria della Salute was begun as a thanks offering. Wars wore down the Republic as well. It fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> intermittently from 1350 to 1381 and destroyed the Genoese fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chioggia">Chioggia</a> in 1380. Defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-cambrai">League of Cambrai</a> at Agnadello on 14 May 1509 put French and imperial troops in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-gritti">Andrea Gritti</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> in July and the mainland was recovered after seven years of war.</p><p>The Ottoman wars took the eastern possessions. After 1453 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> began a series of wars; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a> fell in 1470, and the peace of 24 January 1479 cost Venice <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argos">Argos</a>, Negroponte, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scutari</a> and a tribute of 10,000 ducats a year. Famagusta, the last stronghold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, surrendered after Turkish attack from 1570, and the Turkish commander had the provveditore Marcantonio Bragadin flayed alive. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cretan-war-1645-1669">Cretan War</a> lasted about 25 years and ended with the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Candia</a> on 6 September 1669. On 12 May 1797 the Great Council voted to hand power to a provisional government by 512 votes to ten with five abstentions, although a quorum was lacking. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were rounded up in Venice and deported to extermination camps.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antonio-vivaldi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-vivaldi.jpg" alt="Antonio Vivaldi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antonio-vivaldi">Antonio Vivaldi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vivaldi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The old city and its lagoon were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1987. Venice was a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, painting and music: it was the printing capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by 1500, and Venetian painting is known for its warm colour scale. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-vivaldi">Antonio Vivaldi</a> and Tomaso Albinoni were born there, and the Venetian school of music flourished in the 16th century, when Adrian Willaert worked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">St Mark</a>'s Basilica. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> (1254 to 1324) and Giacomo Casanova (1725 to 1798) were Venetians. English words from Venetian include arsenal, ghetto, gondola, lagoon, lazaret, lido and regatta.</p><p>The city still holds the Venice Biennale, begun in 1895, and the Venice Film Festival, founded in 1932. Buildings from the Republic stand along the Grand Canal, and the Arsenal is still used by the Italian Army. The city also faces flooding. Acqua alta, high water, covers its lowest surfaces; the tide of 13 November 2019 peaked at 1.87 metres, the highest since 1966, and the MOSE barrier was first raised on 3 October 2020. Venice is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a> and of the Metropolitan City of Venice; nearly 50,000 people live in the historic island city and the municipality has about 250,000.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/maoz-tzur">Ma'oz Tzur<span>c. 1200</span></a><a href="/songs/lekha-dodi">Lekha Dodi<span>c. 1550</span></a><a href="/songs/riu-riu-chiu">Ríu Ríu Chíu<span>1556</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Venice is a city of islands in a lagoon at the head of the Adriatic, traditionally founded in 421. As the capital of a maritime republic it grew rich on trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, and, after wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and repeated plague, lost its independence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1797.</em></p>

